aud: fix the ALSA playback ring protocol

Three defects kept the DSP-side `playback` module and the ALSA ring out of
sync, so every stream either failed with EPIPE or delivered nothing. Traced on
an LG OLED65C21LA (o22) by logging the kernel's aplayback_ack against the
module's own process_buf on one timeline.

1. The module was never started or stopped.

   It starts its own output thread as soon as aimc_module_create() returns and
   is never told to stop; alsa_playback.c's trigger only set a local
   `is_started` flag and sent no IPC at all.

     KRN ack: wrOffset 0x01ffc sw_ptr 0 ... rdOffset 0x00400 hw_avail 2048
     KRN aplayback_trigger 649: start     <- ALSA starts once the ring is FULL
     ... but DSP process_buf had been draining that ring the whole time ...

     KRN aplayback_trigger 644: stop
     KRN aplayback_hw_free 754: hma free done  <- ring returned to the HMA pool
     DSP process_buf: wr 0x01ffc rd 0x01c00    <- still reading it, 100 ms later

   So it consumed the buffer while userspace was still prefilling -- the
   hardware pointer ran past the application pointer and the first write
   failed with EPIPE -- and it kept reading the DMA buffer after hw_free()
   released it. It also explains the "playback_output_thread_callback: wrong
   read ptr 0x00000000" logged at construction, before any
   CAPTURE_CMD_SET_BUFFER can have arrived.

   alsa_capture.c:795 and lgsic_mc.c:437 drive the same CAPTURE_CMD_SET_BUFFER
   / CAPTURE_CMD_RESET_BUFFER protocol and both send ADEC_CMD_START/STOP from
   their trigger. alsa_playback.c was the only one that did not. ADEC_CMD_START
   and ADEC_CMD_STOP come from imc/adec_imc_cmd.h, already in scope via
   imc/cmd/module_cmd_capture.h.

2. The write pointer was never announced.

     KRN aplayback_ack 701: called ... appl_ptr:512  buffer_size:2048
     KRN aplayback_ack 701: called ... appl_ptr:1024 buffer_size:2048
     KRN aplayback_ack 701: called ... appl_ptr:1536 buffer_size:2048
     KRN aplayback_ack 701: called ... appl_ptr:2048 buffer_size:2048
     KRN aplayback_trigger 549: start        <- is_started set only HERE
     DSP process_buf: wr 0x00000(0) rd 0x00400(1024)  <- and in all 24 others

   aplayback_ack announced wrOffset only `if (aplayback->is_started)`, but ALSA
   prefills the entire buffer before it issues TRIGGER_START. Every prefill ack
   was gated off and nothing was ever sent, leaving the module at wr == 0.

3. A full ring was indistinguishable from an empty one.

   At TRIGGER_START the open-coded `appl_ptr % buffer_size` aliases a
   completely full ring onto 0 -- the same value that means empty. The module
   read (0 - rdOffset) mod size as the available byte count and burst-drained
   seven 1024-byte chunks in ~1 ms (log timestamps 150 us apart) instead of
   pacing them over 37 ms. That laps the application pointer, so the first
   write returns EPIPE.

Both pointer paths now go through one helper, which announces on every ack
and, when the ring is exactly full, holds back one PERIOD so the value cannot
alias. A period is a whole number of the module's 1024-byte process_buf chunk.
An earlier attempt (patches/not-working/) held back a single FRAME, 4 bytes,
which broke that alignment: the module read a full 1024-byte chunk when only
1020 bytes were available, rdOffset overshot wrOffset by 4, and the modular
arithmetic flipped to ~8188 bytes available -- the same runaway by a different
route. Values observed then were wr 8188, 1020, 3068, always 4 short of a
chunk boundary.

The kaimc.c hunk is a consequence of fix 2. aimc_set_property() allocates with
GFP_KERNEL, and announcing from every ack means calling it from atomic context:
ALSA invokes ops->ack() with the PCM stream lock held and interrupts disabled
(sound/core/pcm_lib.c:2092). LG's original code had the same defect behind the
is_started gate; removing the gate makes it fire on every ack, so the
allocation becomes GFP_ATOMIC. It is small and freed before the function
returns.

Verified on an LG OLED65C21LA: speaker-test runs with zero EPIPE at the
default buffer size, and rdOffset advances at real time.

diff --git a/linux-and-kdriver_okapi-3/kdriver/core/aud/alsa/alsa_playback.c b/linux-and-kdriver_okapi-3/kdriver/core/aud/alsa/alsa_playback.c
index ef3aee29b..3d4834374 100644
--- a/linux-and-kdriver_okapi-3/kdriver/core/aud/alsa/alsa_playback.c
+++ b/linux-and-kdriver_okapi-3/kdriver/core/aud/alsa/alsa_playback.c
@@ -522,6 +522,40 @@ static int aplayback_prepare (struct snd_pcm_substream *substream)
     return 0;
 }
 
+/**
+ * Announce the write pointer to the DSP playback module.
+ *
+ * Two things this gets right that the open-coded versions did not:
+ *
+ * 1. It is called for EVERY ack, not only once the stream is started. ALSA
+ *    prefills the whole buffer before it issues TRIGGER_START, so gating on
+ *    is_started meant the entire prefill was never announced and the module
+ *    sat at wr == 0 for the life of the stream.
+ *
+ * 2. When the ring is exactly full, appl_ptr % buffer_size aliases onto 0 --
+ *    the same value that means "empty". Traced: the module then computed
+ *    (0 - rdOffset) mod size as the available byte count and burst-drained
+ *    seven 1024-byte chunks in ~1 ms instead of pacing them over 37 ms, which
+ *    laps the application pointer and raises XRUN. So in that case hold back
+ *    one PERIOD. A period is a whole number of the module's 1024-byte
+ *    process_buf chunks; an earlier attempt held back a single frame (4 bytes)
+ *    and broke that alignment, leaving the module reading 1024 bytes when only
+ *    1020 were available.
+ */
+static void _aplayback_announce_wroffset (struct snd_pcm_substream *substream)
+{
+    struct snd_pcm_runtime *runtime = substream->runtime;
+    struct aplayback_priv *aplayback = runtime->private_data;
+    snd_pcm_uframes_t frames = runtime->control->appl_ptr;
+
+    if (snd_pcm_playback_hw_avail (runtime) >= (snd_pcm_sframes_t) runtime->buffer_size)
+        frames -= runtime->period_size;
+
+    aplayback->wrOffset = frames_to_bytes (runtime, frames % runtime->buffer_size);
+
+    aimc_set_property (aplayback->uid_playback, "wrOffset=%d", aplayback->wrOffset);
+}
+
 static int aplayback_trigger (struct snd_pcm_substream *substream, int cmd)
 {
     struct aplayback_priv *aplayback = substream->runtime->private_data;
@@ -529,20 +563,27 @@ static int aplayback_trigger (struct snd_pcm_substream *substream, int cmd)
     switch (cmd)
     {
         case SNDRV_PCM_TRIGGER_STOP:
+            /* The DSP playback module runs its own output thread from the
+             * moment aimc_module_create() returns, and nothing here ever told
+             * it to stop. Traced: process_buf kept reading the ring 100 ms
+             * after aplayback_hw_free() had returned it to the HMA pool.
+             * alsa_capture.c:795 and lgsic_mc.c:437 -- same CAPTURE_CMD_*
+             * protocol -- both send this; only playback did not. */
+            aimc_send_command (NULL, ADEC_CMD_STOP, 0, aplayback->uid_playback, NULL, 0);
             info ("stop\n");
             aplayback->is_started = false;
             break;
 
         case SNDRV_PCM_TRIGGER_START:
+            /* Without this the module drains the ring while userspace is
+             * still prefilling it, so by the time ALSA reaches START the
+             * hardware pointer has already run away and the first write
+             * fails with EPIPE (XRUN). */
+            aimc_send_command (NULL, ADEC_CMD_START, 0, aplayback->uid_playback, NULL, 0);
             info ("start\n");
-            // if pcm data exists
-            if (substream->runtime->control->appl_ptr == substream->runtime->buffer_size)
-            {
-                snd_pcm_sframes_t sw_ptr =
-                    substream->runtime->control->appl_ptr % substream->runtime->buffer_size;
-                aplayback->wrOffset = frames_to_bytes (substream->runtime, sw_ptr);
-                aimc_set_property (aplayback->uid_playback, "wrOffset=%d", aplayback->wrOffset);
-            }
+            /* Re-announce: the prefill is already in the ring and the module
+             * must be told how much, with the full-ring case disambiguated. */
+            _aplayback_announce_wroffset (substream);
             aplayback->is_started = true;
             break;
 
@@ -689,16 +730,12 @@ static int aplayback_ack (struct snd_pcm_substream *substream)
     debug ("called runtime->control->appl_ptr:%lu buffer_size:%lu\n", runtime->control->appl_ptr,
         runtime->buffer_size);
 
-    if (aplayback->is_started)
-    {
-        sw_ptr = runtime->control->appl_ptr % runtime->buffer_size;
-        aplayback->wrOffset = frames_to_bytes (substream->runtime, sw_ptr);
-        debug ("(device:%d) wrOffset 0x%05x sw_ptr %4ld buffer_size %ld rdOffset 0x%05x\n",
-            soc_pcm_rtd->cpu_dai->id, aplayback->wrOffset, sw_ptr, runtime->buffer_size,
-            aplayback->rdOffset);
+    _aplayback_announce_wroffset (substream);
 
-        aimc_set_property (aplayback->uid_playback, "wrOffset=%d", aplayback->wrOffset);
-    }
+    sw_ptr = runtime->control->appl_ptr % runtime->buffer_size;
+    debug ("(device:%d) wrOffset 0x%05x sw_ptr %4ld buffer_size %ld rdOffset 0x%05x hw_avail %ld\n",
+        soc_pcm_rtd->cpu_dai->id, aplayback->wrOffset, sw_ptr, runtime->buffer_size,
+        aplayback->rdOffset, snd_pcm_playback_hw_avail (runtime));
 
     return 0;
 }
diff --git a/linux-and-kdriver_okapi-3/kdriver/core/aud/kaimc.c b/linux-and-kdriver_okapi-3/kdriver/core/aud/kaimc.c
index b26f2f1e7..39f5bb464 100644
--- a/linux-and-kdriver_okapi-3/kdriver/core/aud/kaimc.c
+++ b/linux-and-kdriver_okapi-3/kdriver/core/aud/kaimc.c
@@ -1300,7 +1300,16 @@ int aimc_set_property (unsigned int target, char *prop_fmt, ...)
     len = vsnprintf (NULL, 0, prop_fmt, ap);
     va_end (ap);
 
-    param = kmalloc (sizeof (*param) + len + 1, GFP_KERNEL);
+    /* GFP_ATOMIC, not GFP_KERNEL: this is reachable from atomic context.
+     * alsa_playback.c's aplayback_ack() announces wrOffset from here, and
+     * ALSA calls ops->ack() with the PCM stream lock held and interrupts
+     * disabled (sound/core/pcm_lib.c:2092, from __snd_pcm_lib_xfer). A
+     * GFP_KERNEL allocation there may sleep, which is a bug even though the
+     * fast path almost always succeeds without sleeping -- CONFIG_DEBUG_
+     * ATOMIC_SLEEP flags it, and under memory pressure it would really sleep
+     * with interrupts off. The allocation is small and is freed before this
+     * function returns, so drawing on the atomic reserves is cheap. */
+    param = kmalloc (sizeof (*param) + len + 1, GFP_ATOMIC);
     if (!param)
     {
         error ("no mem. %s\n", prop_fmt);
